---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import Setting from "../components/Setting.tsx";
import { ValueSets, Measures } from "../components/Choices.tsx";
import { repo } from "../lib/site";
import { lib } from "../lib/data";
import { assumptionValues } from "../engine/model";
import { charityFigure, modelFigure } from "../engine/figures";
import { formatYears } from "../lib/format";

// The worked example of a charity's figure: the Against Malaria Foundation at the defaults, from the same
// function as its page and the rankings, so the numbers here can't drift from theirs.
const av = assumptionValues(lib.assumptions);
const amf = charityFigure(lib, "against-malaria-foundation", av, (m) => modelFigure(lib, m, av), false);
const amfParts = (() => {
  const parts = (amf?.evaluators.find((e) => e.owner === amf.primary)?.programs[0]?.parts ?? []).filter((p) => p.weight > 0);
  const total = parts.reduce((t, p) => t + p.weight, 0);
  return parts.map((p) => ({ label: p.label, share: `${Math.round((p.weight / total) * 100)}%`, value: formatYears(p.central) })).sort((a, b) => parseFloat(b.share) - parseFloat(a.share));
})();
const amfTotal = formatYears(amf?.central ?? NaN);

// The file-format example at the end: a valid model (about 5.8 healthy years per $1,000 at the defaults).
const example = `{
  "id": "example-nets",
  "name": "Example: bed nets (simplified)",
  "kind": "charity",
  "cause": "global-health",
  "rows": [
    {
      "id": "cost_per_net",
      "label": "Cost of one net, delivered ($)",
      "value": 5,
      "kind": "observed",
      "source": { "title": "The charity's accounts", "url": "https://example.org/accounts" }
    },
    {
      "id": "deaths_per_1000_nets",
      "label": "Children's deaths averted per 1,000 nets",
      "value": { "low": 0.2, "best": 0.5, "high": 1 },
      "kind": "estimate",
      "explainer": "From the trials of nets, scaled to today's malaria deaths where the nets go."
    },
    {
      "id": "deaths_per_dollar",
      "label": "Deaths averted per dollar",
      "formula": "deaths_per_1000_nets / 1000 / cost_per_net"
    },
    {
      "id": "healthy_years_per_dollar",
      "label": "Years of healthy life per dollar",
      "formula": "deaths_per_dollar * $daly_per_child_death"
    }
  ],
  "output": { "row": "healthy_years_per_dollar", "unit": "daly", "label": "Years of healthy life per dollar" }
}`;
const toc = [
  { id: "multipliers", label: "From a model to a charity's figure" },
  { id: "sources", label: "Where models come from" },
  { id: "one-way", label: "Same rules for every model" },
  { id: "healthy-years", label: "One scale" },
  { id: "value-sets", label: "Value sets" },
  { id: "people", label: "People" },
  { id: "animals", label: "Animals" },
  { id: "near-and-far", label: "Near and far" },
  { id: "catastrophic", label: "Catastrophic risks" },
  { id: "evidence", label: "Evidence, age and risk" },
  { id: "credit", label: "How credit is shared" },
  { id: "ranges", label: "Reading the rankings" },
  { id: "files", label: "The model files" },
];
---

<Prose title="How this works" active="method" toc={toc} description="Every charity is a model file, imported from an evaluator or built by us from public evidence, ranked by the good the next $1,000 does, under values you set.">
  <p class="lede">
    The aim is to put every charity in <strong>one comparable, quantitative framework</strong>: one question, <strong>how much good does the next $1,000 you give do?</strong>, answered for each the same way. Each answer is a model, a public file of numbers and formulas. Most come from charity evaluators; the rest we build.
  </p>
  <div class="pros-cons">
    <div>
      <h3 class="pc-h">What it gives you</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Charities from any cause or evaluator side by side.</li>
        <li>Every assumption written down, with its source, and the value judgments yours to change.</li>
        <li>The same rules for everyone, so a gap between two charities comes from their work, not their evaluator's method.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="pc-h">What it costs</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>One number hides its uncertainty; the ranges and warnings are part of the answer.</li>
        <li>What's hard to measure gets only a rough estimate, which favours work that is easy to count.</li>
        <li>Comparing causes needs value judgments, such as a chicken against a person, that no data settles.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="multipliers">From a model to a charity's figure</h2>
  <p>
    Every model is a chain of factors multiplied together, so a charity strong on all of them beats one strong on most by a wide margin, and a tenfold error in any one is a tenfold error in the result. <a href="https://forum.effectivealtruism.org/posts/GzmJ2uiTx4gYhpcQK/effectiveness-is-a-conjunction-of-multipliers">Thomas Kwa</a> shows how these multipliers stack; his commenters add that in honest estimates each is smaller than it first looks. Both show up here: each factor is estimated on its own, with its own range.
  </p>
  <div class="chain" aria-label="A model as a chain of factors multiplied together">
    <span class="chain-t">People reached per $</span><span class="chain-op">×</span>
    <span class="chain-t">Effect on each</span><span class="chain-op">×</span>
    <span class="chain-t">What that effect is worth</span><span class="chain-op">×</span>
    <span class="chain-t">Chance it works</span><span class="chain-op">×</span>
    <span class="chain-t">The share that's the charity's doing</span><span class="chain-op">×</span>
    <span class="chain-t">What the next dollar adds</span><span class="chain-op">=</span>
    <span class="chain-t chain-r">Good per $1,000</span>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Kwa's other multipliers are yours, not the charity's: how much you earn, give and risk. We rank where a given dollar goes.
  </p>
  <p id="itn">
    The three factors often used to compare causes, how big a problem is, how neglected and how tractable, multiply to the same good per extra dollar (<a href="https://80000hours.org/articles/problem-framework/">80,000 Hours</a>), so we don't score them on top: that would count them twice (<a href="https://forum.effectivealtruism.org/posts/Eav7tedvX96Gk2uKE/the-itn-framework-cost-effectiveness-and-cause">Halstead</a>, <a href="https://forum.effectivealtruism.org/posts/MtCAsPMftvJqRBYzr/review-of-itn-critiques">a review of the critiques</a>).
  </p>

  <h3 id="charity-figure">A charity's figure</h3>
  <p>
    A charity with several programmes, or one programme in several countries, gets the spending-weighted average of them, where the split is published. The Against Malaria Foundation at the defaults, for example:
  </p>
  <div class="eq" aria-label={`Against Malaria Foundation: ${amfParts.map((p) => `${p.share} of ${p.value}`).join(" plus ")} is about ${amfTotal}`}>
    {amfParts.map((p, i) => (
      <>
        <span class="eq-term"><b>{p.share}</b> × {p.value}<small>{p.label}</small></span><span class="eq-op">{i === amfParts.length - 1 ? "=" : "+"}</span>
      </>
    ))}
    <span class="eq-term eq-result"><b>{amfTotal}</b><small>the charity</small></span>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each evaluator's figure stands on its own; we never blend evaluators. A charity analysed by several shows each as its own dot, and its number is the newest analysis from the first of them in a fixed order (GiveWell first, ours last).
  </p>
  <p>
    Where an evaluator's models cover less than 90% of the money behind a charity's programmes, the figure still ranks, with a warning naming what's missing. Charities that move money to others (fundraisers, effective-giving groups) are worth what that money does where it goes; where the recipients aren't named, at the cause's portfolio: the charities a published fund for that cause gave to, weighted by its grants.
  </p>
  <p id="theoretical">
    Some models are purely theoretical: they estimate an idea before any charity runs it. <a href="/evaluators/ambitious-impact">Ambitious Impact</a>, for example, estimates programmes when deciding which new charities to start. Where nothing models a charity's actual work since, that estimate is shown, marked purely theoretical, but never ranks: an estimate of an idea says little about the charity running it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources">Where models come from</h2>
  <p id="imported-models">
    <strong>Imported:</strong> every public cost-effectiveness model we found and could read, from <a href="/evaluators/givewell">GiveWell</a>, <a href="/evaluators/ace">Animal Charity Evaluators</a>, the <a href="/evaluators/hli">Happier Lives Institute</a>, <a href="/evaluators/ambitious-impact">Ambitious Impact</a>, <a href="/evaluators/sogive">SoGive</a>, <a href="/evaluators/the-life-you-can-save">The Life You Can Save</a> and <a href="/evaluators">others</a> (the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/inventory`}>inventory</a> lists everything and why anything was left out). Each spreadsheet is traced from its bottom line, and every calculated row must match the sheet, or the build fails.
  </p>
  <p id="our-models">
    <strong>Ours:</strong> where no evaluator has looked, we build the model by the method below.
  </p>

  <h3 id="build">How we build and check a model</h3>
  <p>
    An AI agent follows published instructions: the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.claude/skills/build-cea/SKILL.md`}>build skill</a> for our models, the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.claude/skills/import-cea/SKILL.md`}>import skill</a> for evaluators' and the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.claude/skills/audit-cea/SKILL.md`}>audit skill</a> for the review.
  </p>
  <ol class="build-steps">
    <li><strong>Scope.</strong> Every programme the money pays for, from the charity's accounts, weighted by spending.</li>
    <li><strong>Template.</strong> A fixed template for each kind of work, ending on years of healthy life, with every way the work does good or harm.</li>
    <li><strong>Sources.</strong> A separate AI researcher first gathers the accounts, studies and any evaluations of the charity's own programmes. Every number is traced to a source, or stated as a judgment with a range and the studies it rests on.</li>
    <li><strong>Automatic checks.</strong> Scripts test units, formulas, own copies of shared figures, the standard steps and the simulation; any failure stops the model before the audit.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent audit.</strong> A fresh AI reviewer, given the model and its write-up but not the builder's notes, fetches the key sources and checks each number, looking for double counting, missing adjustments and unit errors. The builder fixes what it finds and a new reviewer checks those fixes, until no serious finding is left.</li>
    <li><strong>Publish.</strong> Only once the latest audit, of the model exactly as it is, finds nothing serious; a test checks this. Numbers in the text are read from the model, so they change when it does.</li>
  </ol>
  <p id="templates">
    <strong>Templates.</strong> Every model of one kind of work starts from the same <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/.claude/skills/build-cea/templates`}>template</a>, so any two read side by side. Each has the same <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.claude/skills/build-cea/references/templates.md`}>sections</a>: the cost, the reach (people, animals or tonnes), the effect on each from the evidence, the adjustments (how sure the evidence is, what would have happened anyway, the charity's share of the credit), the outcome, and the steps to years of healthy life.
 
  </p>
  <p>
    Every audit round is public in the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/audits`}>audits folder</a>, with the <a href="/changelog">rules version</a> it checked. Built blind for five charities evaluators have analysed, two landed within 3× of the evaluator (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/eval-skills.md`}>details</a>).
  </p>
  <h2 id="one-way">Same rules for every model</h2>
  <p id="views">
    The same rules apply to every model, ours and the evaluators'. We keep each evaluator's analysis as published, so it still reproduces, but rank our version: the site's shared figures and your values in place of the evaluator's own. Where evaluators use different numbers for the same change (a hen out of a cage, a meal replaced), every model uses one shared figure. A row where we use a different number is marked "changed for consistency", and each model page shows, step by step, how the evaluator's final figure becomes ours.
  </p>

  <h3 id="costs-to-others">Costs to others, and gifts in kind</h3>
  <p>
    A rule a charity helps win costs those who must comply, and public money it wins costs those who pay taxes: both count against the good, at what that money means to the people who lose it. Goods and services given in kind count at what they are worth to those who get them, usually less than the same money in cash.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthy-years">One scale: years of healthy life</h2>
  <p>
    Every model ends on one scale: <strong>years of healthy life</strong>, one person living one more year in full health (a DALY averted). Evaluators count in other units, so each is converted:
  </p>
  <div class="units">
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">Doubled income for a year</span><span class="unit-by">GiveWell</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A point of life satisfaction for a year</span><span class="unit-by">Happier Lives Institute</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A day of disabling pain, in chickens</span><span class="unit-by">Animal evaluators</span></div>
    <div class="unit"><span class="unit-from">A tonne of CO₂ kept out of the air</span><span class="unit-by">Climate evaluators</span></div>
    <div class="unit-to">→ years of healthy life</div>
  </div>
  <h2 id="value-sets">Value sets</h2>
  <p>
    Each set is a few of the values below, chosen together for one point of view. Pick one here or in Your values; change any slider afterwards and you have your own set.
  </p>
  <ValueSets client:load />

  <h2 id="people">People</h2>
  <Setting client:load ids={["uov_per_daly", "daly_per_child_death"]} />
  <p class="small muted">
    Deaths at other ages count in GiveWell's proportions by age: a school-age child's a little more, an adult's over 50 much less.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wellbeing">Life satisfaction</h3>
  <p>
    The default makes a year of depression one standard deviation milder count the same through life satisfaction as through health researchers' disability weights: a point of life satisfaction for a year is about 24 healthy days.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["daly_per_wellby"]} />

  <h2 id="animals">Animals</h2>
  <p>
    Days of pain are counted in chickens, other species against a chicken by Rethink Priorities' estimates of how much each can suffer: a pig, cow, dog or cat as 1.6 chickens, a fish 0.2, a shrimp 0.1. Then two values: how many chickens' suffering equals a person's, and what a day of disabling pain costs in healthy time: about 10 hours by how health surveys weigh the worst human pain, up to 240 if pain counts by how bad it feels.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["chicken_vs_person", "daly_per_sad", "pain_free_time_good"]} />
  <p class="small muted">
    The default of 10,000 chickens is what people's own spending on chicken welfare implies; researchers who rate how much a chicken can suffer put it at 3.
  </p>

  <h2 id="near-and-far">Near and far</h2>
  <p>
    Future good is discounted, a wait before it starts included. Where work changes how many people or animals ever live, the suffering in those lives always counts and their good years as much as you set, 0% by default. A life saved changes that too: parents have fewer later births, the survivor has children, and all of them eat animals. By default a life counts the same wherever it is; set where you live and a discount to count lives further away less.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["distance_discount", "home_country", "discount_rate", "never_started_weight"]} />

  <h2 id="catastrophic">Catastrophic risks</h2>
  <p>
    Work on AI, pandemics and nuclear war is valued by how much it cuts the chance of a catastrophe this century. How likely each catastrophe is, is yours to set. The default for AI is the median answer of 1,321 AI researchers in 2023; those for pandemics and nuclear war are the median expert forecasts in a 2022 forecasting tournament.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["ai_catastrophe_chance", "bio_catastrophe_chance", "nuclear_catastrophe_chance"]} />

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence, age and risk</h2>
  <h3 id="estimate-age">Old estimates</h3>
  <p>
    Evaluators re-check what they recommend every year or two; an estimate nobody renewed is less likely to hold. Estimates over 5 years old are left out unless you switch them back on. At 5% a year a 2-year-old estimate counts 91%, a 4-year-old one 82%.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["ignore_old_estimates", "estimate_age_discount"]} />

  <h3 id="missing-data">Results nobody measured</h3>
  <p>
    A result counts in full where the charity's own count of who it reached meets an effect measured in trials or its own controlled evaluation. Anything resting on judgment, projections or research on other delivery counts less. GiveWell's top charities are measured; idea-stage and most campaign estimates are not.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["missing_data_discount"]} />

  <h3 id="side-effects">Side effects</h3>
  <p>
    Besides what a programme is for, it often does other good or harm: to people around those it helps, to other health outcomes, or by pushing work elsewhere. Each is an estimate in the model like any other number, and this sets how much they count.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["side_effect_weight"]} />

  <h3 id="risk">Risk and long shots</h3>
  <p>
    No result is known exactly. For every model we draw its uncertain inputs 1,000 times, each across its source's range or a default spread where none is given, with your values held fixed, and keep the spread of results. Where a model rests on one event happening at all (a new charity succeeding, a law passing), that event is drawn as happens or doesn't, so a long shot's outcomes are mostly nothing and sometimes a lot.
  </p>
  <figure class="risk-fig">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 460 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
      <title id="risk-title">A sure thing's outcomes cluster around its best guess; a 1-in-4 long shot's outcomes are nothing three times in four and four times the best guess once in four</title>
      <text x="0" y="22" class="rk-l">Sure thing</text>
      <path d="M170 50 C 180 50, 183 14, 190 14 C 197 14, 200 50, 210 50 Z" class="rk-sure" />
      <text x="0" y="102" class="rk-l">1-in-4 long shot</text>
      <rect x="120" y="66" width="18" height="44" class="rk-shot" />
      <path d="M333 110 C 350 110, 360 92, 373 92 C 386 92, 396 110, 413 110 Z" class="rk-shot" />
      <text x="129" y="126" text-anchor="middle" class="rk-t">nothing, 3 in 4</text>
      <text x="373" y="126" text-anchor="middle" class="rk-t">4× best guess, 1 in 4</text>
      <line x1="190" y1="6" x2="190" y2="140" class="rk-best" />
      <text x="190" y="148" text-anchor="middle" class="rk-t">best guess (both)</text>
    </svg>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Both have the same best guess, which counts every chance at its odds: a 1-in-4 chance of 4 is 1. The slider sets how much risk you accept that the real impact is lower than the figure counted. At 20%, the real impact is lower one time in five; at 80%, four times in five.
  </p>
  <div class="risk-table" role="table" aria-label="The two examples at three risk settings">
    <div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Accept a risk of</span><span role="columnheader">20%</span><span role="columnheader">50% (default)</span><span role="columnheader">80%</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Sure thing</span><span role="cell">a little below best</span><span role="cell">about best</span><span role="cell">a little above</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Long shot</span><span role="cell">0</span><span role="cell">0</span><span role="cell">4× best</span></div>
  </div>
  <p id="average">
    <strong>Or count the average.</strong> Instead of a point in the range, each charity can count at its average result: every possible outcome weighed by its odds. A long shot then counts at its odds times its payoff, the sure thing at about its best guess. This is the expected value most evaluators say they maximise, and the value sets for evidence, high risk, the long run and AI use it. For most health charities the average is about 15% above the best guess; for catastrophic-risk charities, whose rare successes are very large, it is about 2.7 times.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["risk_quantile"]} />
  <p id="hits">
    <a href="https://coefficientgiving.org/research/hits-based-giving/">Hits-based giving</a> (Coefficient Giving, formerly Open Philanthropy) argues for accepting that most bets fail when the few that succeed do far more. At the default 50%, a long shot with less than even odds counts as 0.
  </p>

  <h3 id="climate">Climate</h3>
  <p>
    A tonne of CO₂ is valued by the deaths it causes: about 1.9 per 10,000 tonnes (Rethink Priorities), three quarters from the air pollution of burning fuel and a quarter from heat. Pollution deaths come at once, but only tonnes from burning less fuel avert them; heat deaths come about 60 years later and are discounted. Damage that isn't death, such as lost harvests, property and work, adds 60% more (30–110%, the EPA's 2023 split of the social cost of carbon), counted as a side effect.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["deaths_per_tco2e"]} />

  <h3 id="next-dollar">The next dollar, and reforms</h3>
  <p>
    Most models estimate the next grant or the cost of one more unit of work. Some divide everything an organisation achieved by everything it spent: an average, and the next dollar usually does less. How much less is estimated charity by charity: how much room it has for more money, what the next dollar buys, and how well it is run. A won reform counts only for the years before it would have come anyway, after the wait until it takes effect.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["win_years_counted"]} />

  <h2 id="credit">How credit is shared</h2>
  <p>
    Two settings, one test: would this party have paid or acted without the charity? Funders who would have paid anyway are co-funders, and credit can be split by who paid for what. Donors, governments and companies that act only because of the charity share the good with it: the charity gets 30% by default. Credit then roughly adds up to the whole.
  </p>
  <Setting client:load ids={["resource_shares", "mobiliser_share"]} />

  <h2 id="ranges">Reading the rankings</h2>
  <figure class="range-fig">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 440 64" role="img" aria-labelledby="range-title">
      <title id="range-title">A result's dot on its bar: the dot is the figure at your risk setting, the bar runs from 5% to 95% of the simulated outcomes</title>
      <rect x="70" y="24" width="300" height="10" rx="5" class="rf-bar" />
      <circle cx="190" cy="29" r="8" class="rf-dot" />
      <text x="70" y="56" class="rf-t">1 in 20 lower</text>
      <text x="370" y="56" text-anchor="end" class="rf-t">1 in 20 higher</text>
      <text x="190" y="14" text-anchor="middle" class="rf-t">the figure counted</text>
    </svg>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Results span about a millionfold, so the rankings use a log scale. Each charity sits at its dot; the bar is the simulated range from 5% to 95%. A log scale can't show zero or harm, so results at or below zero sit in the ≤0 zone at its left, and a range that reaches them shows a stub there, its bar starting where the simulated results turn positive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The list is sorted by the dot but has no rank numbers: neighbours' ranges usually overlap, so which of two close charities comes first is often a guess. The order still shows what the evidence supports: gaps of tenfold and more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a result is close to zero, a net of large opposite effects, the risk setting doesn't move it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A warning sign marks a charity whose range spans over 100×, whose uncertainty is mostly judgment, whose analysis is over 18 months old, whose models leave programmes out, whose simulation can't be relied on, or whose model is purely theoretical. On each model page every number shows its share of the uncertainty.
  </p>

  <h3 id="measures">Which measure you see</h3>
  <p>
    The rankings can show the same figures in three measures. Each is the healthy-year figure times one rate, so the order never changes, only the numbers:
  </p>
  <Measures client:load />

  <h2 id="files">The model files</h2>
  <p>
    Each model is one JSON file in the <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/models`}>repository</a>: a flat list of rows, like the cells of a spreadsheet. A row either holds a number (<code>value</code>, with a range where the source gives one, its <code>kind</code> and its source) or calculates one (<code>formula</code>, naming other rows by their <code>id</code>). <code>output</code> names the row the ranking reads and its unit. A simplified example:
  </p>
  <pre class="json"><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <p id="judgments">
    A row's <code>kind</code> is <strong>observed</strong>, <strong>estimate</strong> or <strong>judgment</strong>. A judgment that concerns only a few models is a fixed figure rather than a slider, such as how long until a tonne's heat deaths come, stand-ins for species Rethink Priorities hasn't estimated, or the share of corporate pledges companies keep; each carries a range and links to its source from the model trees. Each model's history page shows every change.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rows are flat rather than nested because one number often feeds several others (a quarter of all rows do), as in a spreadsheet. The model page draws the tree from the formulas, starting at the output:
  </p>
  <ul class="tree" aria-label="The example as a tree">
    <li><code>healthy_years_per_dollar</code>
      <ul>
        <li><code>deaths_per_dollar</code>
          <ul>
            <li><code>deaths_per_1000_nets</code> <span>0.2 to 1, best 0.5</span></li>
            <li><code>cost_per_net</code> <span>$5</span></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><code>$daly_per_child_death</code> <span>your value</span></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
